## Chat Naming System

Structured names keep parallel pipeline/grade sessions identifiable. Use **exactly** this pattern everywhere: Task `description`, first line of subagent prompt, and your one-line status to the user.

```
{namespace} · {scope} · {stage}
```

| Segment | Values | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `namespace` | `coach` · `pipeline` · `write` · `grade` | `pipeline` |
| `scope` | `{year}/{company-slug}/{position-slug}` or topic slug | `2027/clipboard/engineering-intern` |
| `stage` | `run` · `parse` · `persona` · `iter-{n}` · `fit` · `ship` · `screen` · `final` · `tracker` · topic | `run` |

**Examples**

- `pipeline · 2027/clipboard/engineering-intern · run` — full JD pipeline
- `grade · 2027/anduril/software-engineer-intern · final` — post-loop grade pass
- `write · 2027/ctgt/software-engineering-intern · iter-4` — writer grading-response (pipeline spawns this)
- `coach · competitive · anduril` — multi-position competitiveness synthesis (you, not a subagent title)

**Rules:** lowercase namespaces; `/` in scope (not spaces); no company legal suffixes in slug; stage is the finest-grained step you are on. Pipeline orchestrator owns `write` and `grade` spawn names during the loop.

